# `vscode/` is OpenIDE's canonical source tree. Its build artifacts are
# excluded through vscode/.gitignore; the source itself is never ignored.
/vscode/.git.upstream-backup/
/vscode/*.bak
/vscode/resources/server/*.bak
/vscode/cli/*.tgz
/vscode-*
VS*/*
VSCode*
VSCodium*
.DS_Store
*.env
assets/
build/linux/appimage/out
build/linux/appimage/pkg2appimage.AppDir
build/linux/appimage/pkg2appimage-*.AppImage
build/linux/appimage/pkg2appimage.AppImage
build/linux/appimage/squashfs-root
build/linux/appimage/VSCodium
build/windows/msi/releasedir
build/windows/msi/Files*.wxs
build/windows/msi/Files*.wixobj
sourcemaps/
stores/snapcraft/insider/*.snap
stores/snapcraft/stable/*.snap
font-size/node_modules/
font-size/generate-css.js

# OpenIDE: nix-build symlinks
result
result-*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build artifacts and runtime state.
#
# None of this is source: it is regenerated. Without these rules the first
# commit of the tree carried 1.3 GB of `.build/` (the AppImage's AppDir, with a
# whole node_modules inside) and 719 MB of `.openide/` indexes, which are also
# derived from the user's code and have no reason to travel to a remote.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.build/
/tmp/
/vendor/

# Agent state in the workspace: the indexes are derived and heavy; what is
# written by hand (skills, plans, MEMORY.md) is versioned.
#
# `**/` on purpose, not `/`: the indexer writes its `.openide/` next to the
# folder it opened, and opening a subfolder of the repository left 34 MB of
# indexes outside these rules (`vscode/src/vs/workbench/browser/.openide/`). A
# pattern with a `/` in the middle is anchored to the root by itself, so
# dropping the leading anchor is not enough: any depth has to be requested.
**/.openide/memory-indexes/
**/.openide/codegraph/

# Generated by the product, not written for it. `.openide/plans/` is where Plans
# mode writes (`openideChatTranscriptTools.ts`), so these are this repository's
# own dogfooding output: drafts of features, some shipped, some abandoned, all
# written for one reader. `MEMORY.md` is the agent's index of them. They were
# tracked while the repository was private; publishing them would put a
# half-decided roadmap in front of anyone who clones it -- and ignoring the path
# also keeps contributors from committing their own plans. They stay on disk.
# `.openide/skills/` is not ignored: those are real product artifacts.
.openide/plans/
.openide/MEMORY.md

# Same rule, other locations. `dev/design-*.md` are design notes written for one reader (one of
# them is a prompt to paste into another assistant), and `docs/i18n-pendientes.md` is a generated
# inventory of hardcoded Spanish strings -- a work list, not documentation. Nothing links to any
# of them.
dev/design-*.md
dev/prompt-*.md
docs/i18n-pendientes.md

# Private keys. A committed signing key is not "deleted" by a following commit:
# it stays in the history and has to be rotated. The rule is deliberately broad:
# ignoring too much is cheaper than filtering once.
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.key

# Agent handoffs are local; durable Markdown notes may be versioned.
**/.openide/memory/sessions/
